    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders sit at 10.4% of volume with 4 sets, and the strength index has not moved for at least two consecutive periods. While not critically under-trained relative to chest and back, the stagnation suggests the current stimulus is insufficient."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Add one extra set each to biceps and triceps next period, bringing both to 4 sets, to push past the current stalling point on those groups. Keep the load the same but aim for one additional rep per set to create a clear overload signal.",
    "For shoulders, try increasing the working weight by a small increment on your primary shoulder exercise rather than adding sets — the volume (4 sets) is reasonable, so a load nudge is the logical next lever to break the flat strength index.",
    "On chest, back, and legs, where the index is trending upward, maintain your current approach but continue applying small, consistent load or rep increases each session. These groups are close to the on-track threshold and just need steady pressure to cross it.",
    "Log your session weights and reps consistently each workout so that next period's strength index can capture the impact of these changes clearly and you can confirm whether the adjustments are working."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Stalling period with small positive strength trends on chest, back, and legs but flat indices on shoulders, biceps, and triceps; priority for next period is adding overload stimulus to the smaller muscle groups while maintaining momentum on the larger ones."
}
